{% from "macros.j2" import q, plain with context %}
<section class="panel" id="tab-genetic" data-name="genetic">
{{ plain('genetic') }}
{% set GS = GT.summary %}{% set CK = GS.thresholds.check %}
<h2>Genetics first: risk variant, gene, cell and direction {{ q('genetic') }}</h2>
<p class="lede">The other three lists start from what changes in inflamed biopsies, which mixes cause and consequence: a gene can rise only because the tissue is inflamed. This list starts from inherited risk variants, which come before the disease. For each of {{ GS.signals }} independent UC risk signals it asks which gene the risk allele changes, in which cell, and in which direction, so that the direction says whether to inhibit or to restore. The gene and cell are <strong>predicted</strong> from DNA sequence by AlphaGenome and checked against measured eQTL; they are not measurements.</p>
<div class="kpis">
  <div class="kpi"><b>{{ GS.signals }}</b><span>independent risk signals ({{ GS.signals_ibd_only }} from IBD GWAS with a UC association, {{ GS.signals_preprint_only }} only in a 2025 preprint)</span></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>{{ GS.signals_with_gene }}</b><span>signals where a gene could be assigned ({{ GS.signals_with_confident_gene }} confident)</span></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>{{ GS.genes }}</b><span>genes on this list</span></div>
  <div class="kpi"><b>{{ "{:,}".format(GS.ag2_predictions) }}</b><span>cell-type predictions in {{ GS.conditions | length }} colon and blood cell types</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note bad"><strong>The main result is how short this list is.</strong> A gene could be assigned to {{ GS.signals_with_gene }} of {{ GS.signals }} risk signals. For the others the predicted effects are too small, or no single gene stands out, so the sequence model does not say which gene the risk variant acts on. The list is a small set of well-supported hypotheses, not a map of UC genetics.</div>
<div class="howbox"><h4>How the list is built</h4><ul>
<li><strong>Risk signals.</strong> Fine-mapped credible sets of Open Targets 26.09 for five UC GWAS (Liu 2023, de Lange 2017, FinnGen R12, Million Veteran Program, UK Biobank), several independent signals per locus allowed, MHC excluded. Credible sets of IBD GWAS are added only when one of their variants is associated with UC (p &lt; 10⁻⁴). Loci found only in the UC meta-analysis of Lyu et al. 2025, a <strong>preprint that is not peer reviewed</strong>, are fine-mapped approximately and flagged.</li>
<li><strong>Effect of each variant.</strong> AlphaGenome Atlas (colon tissue, blood T cells, blood monocytes) for every credible-set variant, AlphaGenome 2 single-cell expression for the most likely variants of each signal.</li>
<li><strong>Which gene.</strong> A gene is assigned to a variant in a cell or tissue only when it is the gene with the largest predicted change there, at least {{ "%.0f" | format(GS.thresholds.ratio) }} times the next gene, and the change is as large as those of measured eQTL. Checked on fine-mapped eQTL, the gene picked this way is the measured eQTL gene in {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * CK.atlas.right_gene) }} % of cases in tissue ({{ CK.atlas.pairs }} eQTL) and {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * CK.ag2.right_gene) }} % in cell types ({{ CK.ag2.pairs }}), with the right direction in {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * CK.atlas.right_direction) }} % and {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * CK.ag2.right_direction) }} %. Taking the top gene without these conditions is right in only {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * CK.top_gene_alone.atlas) }} % and {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * CK.top_gene_alone.ag2) }} %.</li>
<li><strong>A first version was wrong and is not shown.</strong> It kept every gene whose predicted change was large enough for the direction of a known eQTL to be right. That does not say which gene: about five genes per variant passed, 316 genes in all, and their direction agreed with Mendelian randomisation only at chance.</li>
<li><strong>Tiers.</strong> <em>Confident</em>: the variants that give this effect hold at least half of the signal's causal probability. <em>Likely</em>: they hold 10 to 50 %.</li>
<li><strong>Cross-check with the rest of the atlas.</strong> Points: tier (3 or 2), Mendelian randomisation agreeing (+2) or disagreeing (−2) with the predicted direction, the gene changing in inflamed biopsies in the same direction (+1), the predicted cell being the cell family that makes the gene in single cells (+1), and a UC-specific, peer-reviewed signal (+1). Group A: confident and at least 5 points.</li>
<li><strong>Direction.</strong> Risk allele raises the gene: inhibit. Risk allele lowers it: activate or restore.</li></ul></div>
{% for grp, title in [("A", "Group A: confident, supported by other evidence"), ("B", "Group B: confident"), ("C", "Group C: likely (the causal variant is uncertain)")] %}
{% set rows = GT.rows | selectattr("group", "equalto", grp) | list %}{% if rows %}
<h3>{{ title }} ({{ rows | length }})</h3>
<div class="tw"><table class="t25"><thead><tr><th>Gene</th><th>Risk allele</th><th>Hypothesis</th><th>Cell or tissue (predicted effect)</th><th>Tier</th><th class="num">Causal prob.</th><th>Most likely variant</th><th>Signal (GWAS, lead p)</th><th>In biopsies</th><th>MR</th><th>Drug</th><th class="num">Points</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for r in rows %}<tr{% if r.evidence_ids %} class="claim" data-evidence="{{ r.evidence_ids | evattr }}"{% endif %}><td><strong>{{ r.gene }}</strong>{% if r.in_lists %}<br><span class="tag">{{ r.in_lists }}</span>{% endif %}</td><td>{{ r.risk_allele }}</td><td>{{ r.hypothesis }}</td><td class="wrap">{{ r.contexts }}</td><td>{{ r.tier }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.pip_support }}</td><td>{{ r.top_rsid }} ({{ r.top_pip }})</td><td class="wrap">{{ r.gwas }}{% if r.preprint_only %} <span class="tag warn">preprint</span>{% endif %}{% if r.ibd_signal %} <span class="tag">IBD signal</span>{% endif %}, {{ r.lead_p }}</td><td class="wrap">{{ r.tissue }}</td><td class="wrap">{{ r.mr }}</td><td class="wrap">{{ r.drug }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.points }}</td><td>{{ r.evidence_ids | cite | safe }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>{% endif %}{% endfor %}
<div class="note"><strong>How to read this.</strong> A row is a hypothesis about a mechanism, not a validated target. The gene and cell come from a sequence model: strong predictions were right in the checks above, but a credible set often holds several variants and a risk variant can act in a cell state that the model does not cover (stimulated immune cells, inflamed epithelium). "Causal prob." is the share of the signal's posterior probability held by the variants that give this effect. {{ GS.signals - GS.signals_with_gene }} of the {{ GS.signals }} signals have no assigned gene and stay unexplained here. A gene missing from this list is not evidence against it: IL10 shares its risk variant with IL19, which the model scores higher, so IL10 is not assigned although Mendelian randomisation supports it. Signals marked "preprint" rest on Lyu et al. 2025 (medRxiv), which has not been peer reviewed. AlphaGenome outputs are for non-commercial research only and not for clinical decisions (AlphaGenome Terms of Service); unlike the rest of this report, they are not offered under CC BY 4.0.</div>
</section>
